<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enrico Pescatore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry, Count of Malta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enrico Pescatore was a Genoese adventurer and corsair who held the title Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, with Genoese backing, he occupied much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> around 1206 in defiance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which had bought the claim to the island. Venice drove him out in a war lasting until about 1211–1212 and then colonised the island. He later served Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> as admiral.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name was Enrico da Castello, and the records consider him a Genoese of the noble da Castello family; il Pescatore, the Fisherman, was the name he went by among corsairs. He was the son-in-law of Guglielmo Grasso, the previous holder of the title Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, which Tancred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> had created for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaritus-of-brindisi">Margaritus of Brindisi</a>. His acquisition of the title through that marriage is described as irregular. He also held Malea in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1204 the Pisans occupied Syracuse, which Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> had granted to the Genoese, and the Genoese turned to Pescatore to retake it. Sailing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> with 20 ships and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, he captured two large Pisan ships off the port and took the city after seven days with the help of Alamanno da Costa. In July 1205 he was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, where his force surprised the Pisan Count Benedetto; more than 70 Pisans died. He became one of the most influential members of the government of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> had been allotted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a>, who sold his rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. Venice was not ready to enforce its control, and from 1206 Pescatore held large parts of the island until Venice sent troops and pushed him out a few years later. The Genoese kept Chania, and Alamanno da Costa took up a campaign against Crete.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pescatore later commanded imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and in 1225 he carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-jerusalem">Isabella II of Jerusalem</a> to her marriage to Emperor Frederick II. In 1228 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a> with 40 galleys carrying Frederick on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, a crusade the Church did not recognise. He died around 1230 on a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. The troubadour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peire-vidal">Peire Vidal</a> composed sirventes in his honour during a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry, Count of Malta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Genoese adventurer <b>Enrico Pescatore</b> occupied the island first, and Venice only took control around 1211-1212, sending colonists who received land in return for military service.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-crete">Venetian Crete</a></li></ul></section>
